One common way this occurs is through **Path Traversal** vulnerabilities, specifically when using the `java.io.File` API without validating user input. This allows an attacker to access files outside of the intended directory (like `/etc/passwd` on Linux).

```java
import java.io.*;

public class FileServer {
    // This method is vulnerable to Path Traversal attacks
    public void serveFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        File directory = new File("/var/www/html/uploads/");
        // The user-provided filename is concatenated directly
        File fileToServe = new File(directory, filename);

        // If filename is "../../../etc/passwd", the attacker can read system files
        byte[] content = java.nio.file.Files.readAllBytes(fileToServe.toPath());
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
```

